WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.180
THE DARK SIDE OF SKILLSHARE:
YouTubers sold out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.460
Basically every infotainment video on YouTube&nbsp;
pitches Skillshare as some sort of godly resource.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.920
Do you want to learn how to code? Skillshare. Do&nbsp;
you want to learn how to animate? Skillshare. Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.160
you want to just learn how to learn? Skillshare.&nbsp;
At this point, Skillshare isn’t even seen as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.740
a learning platform. It’s seen as the thing&nbsp;
productive people do. Clearly, Skillshare has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:41.580
done a phenomenal job when it comes to branding,&nbsp;
marketing, and positioning. But, the same cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:48.660
be said about the platform itself. Skillshare&nbsp;
is not only a mediocre platform, it’s a negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.960
platform, and that’s not my opinion. That’s the&nbsp;
overwhelming opinion of everyone who ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:01.860
using the platform. On Trust Pilot, for example,&nbsp;
Skillshare has a horrendous average rating of 1.7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:09.360
stars with 32% of reviews being 1 star ratings.&nbsp;
And that’s despite a massive upward skew from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:16.500
YouTubers. For example, let’s say I’m watching Ali&nbsp;
Abdaal, who is an exceptional YouTuber, and I hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.680
him pitch his Skillshare course. If I go checkout&nbsp;
his Skillshare course, I’ll probably get exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.860
what I’m looking for which may lead to a glowing&nbsp;
5 star review. But, this isn’t really a testament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:35.700
as to how good Skillshare is as a platform. It’s&nbsp;
really just a review of Ali Abdaal or Nathaniel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.580
Drew or Marques Brownlee. If it weren’t for these&nbsp;
select YouTubers, Skillshare’s average rating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:47.940
would probably be closer to that of PayPal. But,&nbsp;
why are people so upset in the first place? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.460
aside from a history of slimy sales practices,&nbsp;
Skillshare simply doesn’t offer the value that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.800
YouTubers say it does. At the end of the day,&nbsp;
it’s really just a worse version of YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:05.040
that costs $32 per month or a $168 per year.&nbsp;
Instead of getting the monthly subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.160
you can get a YouTube premium family plan for&nbsp;
6 people and have $9 per month to spare. Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:18.120
you could get a Premium Netflix subscription for 4&nbsp;
people and still save $12 per month to spare. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.640
here’s the truth about Skillshare and why it&nbsp;
doesn’t live up to all the social media hype.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:31.140
WORSE THAN YOUTUBE:
Likely the biggest complaint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:38.340
about Skillshare is that it’s not actually better&nbsp;
than YouTube. At best, it’s able to match YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:44.040
and this is true for basically any topic that&nbsp;
you want to learn about. Take Swift coding for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.900
example. If you’re not familiar with Swift, it’s&nbsp;
the coding language that you have to use for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:55.620
native iOS development, so it’s a pretty relevant&nbsp;
skill. Yet, if you search up Swift on Skillshare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.800
you don’t get much more than 50 results. And&nbsp;
out of these results, only 3 of them are actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.140
courses on Swift that last 30 plus hours.&nbsp;
Most of the results are just introduction or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:13.800
feature videos. And the remaining results? Well,&nbsp;
they often don’t even have anything to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.320
Swift. We have a video about the principles of&nbsp;
remote work, a video about organizing your life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:25.200
a video about captivation marketing, and&nbsp;
a video about Adobe illustrator. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:32.100
26 out of the 54 results, didn’t have anything&nbsp;
to do about Swift or iOS development at all. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.920
at least there’s three good courses right. Those&nbsp;
alone should be worth a subscription right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.160
that’s what you might think until you take a look&nbsp;
at what YouTube has to offer. CodeWithChris alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:52.080
has 536 videos all about iOS development and many&nbsp;
of these are over 3 hours long. And his videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.400
don’t just teach you how to write a function&nbsp;
or class either. They teach you how to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.920
a feature completely functional from beginning to&nbsp;
end. How to make a custom login page using Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.040
Authentication. How to link up your iOS app to&nbsp;
a cloud database. How to make API requests to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:13.620
implement external functionaily into your apps.&nbsp;
CodeWithChris’ channel literally has more content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:20.160
about Swift than the entirety of Skillshare. And&nbsp;
he’s just one channel. There’s dozens more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:27.600
iOS Academy. iOS Academy has a total of 703&nbsp;
videos covering everything that Chris covers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.240
but he takes it even one step further. He has&nbsp;
these massive series where he builds full on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.820
apps from beginning to end. For example, he’s&nbsp;
currently making a Rick &amp; Morty app completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:47.220
from scratch that has a total of 45 parts. This&nbsp;
series alone has over 10 hours of content. We also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:54.540
have Sean Allen, Paul Hudson, Code Pro, Rebeloper,&nbsp;
Lets Build That App, and so many more in just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:00.900
little niche of Swift. This same argument can&nbsp;
be made for learning Javascript or animation or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:06.900
motion graphics or cooking or basically any&nbsp;
skill you can think of. But none of this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:12.780
really surprising. YouTube is the biggest video&nbsp;
hosting platform in the world. On Skillshare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:19.380
there’s a total of 30,000 courses. On YouTube,&nbsp;
there’s 30,000 hours of content being uploaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:26.820
every single hour. So, naturally, YouTube will&nbsp;
have to most indepth, most comprehensive videos on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:33.840
any niche. And ironically, this is true for even&nbsp;
the YouTubers that pitch Skillshare. Ali Abdaal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.480
for example has a total of maybe 30 hours of&nbsp;
content on Skillshare. Meanwhile, on YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.300
he has hundreds of hours worth of content. We&nbsp;
should also note that Skillshare doesn’t have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.760
sort of vetting process in terms of who can post&nbsp;
content. So, basically anyone can post content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:56.160
on Skillshare just like on YouTube meaning that&nbsp;
the content on Skillshare isn’t necessarily more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:02.400
credible, valuable, or informative. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
there’s plenty of extraordinarily talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.740
teachers on YouTube itself. Most of the coding&nbsp;
channels, for example, or run by former or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.900
current FAANG software engineers. So, even if we&nbsp;
take the cost of Skillshare out of the equation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:18.480
you’re still better off using YouTube. Not only&nbsp;
does Skillshare lack any sort of distinguishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.400
factor, but YouTube destroys Skillshare in&nbsp;
terms of the amount and depth of content.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.360
SNAKEOIL SALESMEN:
Given Skillshare’s glowing reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:36.120
on YouTube, you would think that they’re experts&nbsp;
at public relations and branding. And in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:42.300
customer acquisition they are, but if we take a&nbsp;
look at customer experience, it’s a wholenother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:48.480
story. Honestly, this is pretty surprising. Though&nbsp;
I don’t think that Skillshare is worth the money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.980
I would’ve expected Skillshare to be on top of&nbsp;
their game when it comes to customer satisfaction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:58.740
but reading through these trust pilot reviews,&nbsp;
that doesn’t seem to be the case. You know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:04.560
YouTubers always pitch Skillshare as this risk&nbsp;
free service. You can just try it for 30 days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:11.340
and cancel with no strings attached. This idea is&nbsp;
plastered all over their website as well. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:17.400
in reality, the trial process isn’t what it&nbsp;
seems. D Stevens, for example, claims that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:23.160
he was charged for a one year subscription after&nbsp;
just 2 weeks of trial. At first, Skillshare didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:28.500
even reply to any of his messages. But, even&nbsp;
when they did reply, all they had to say was:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:34.440
“you have 14 days to cancel from the day you sign&nbsp;
up!” This likely seems confusing at first glance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:40.680
but when you read the fine print, it all makes&nbsp;
sense. Basically, you do indeed get one month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:46.020
for free. But, you get charged for the following&nbsp;
12 month period before the trial month even ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:52.080
This is of course stated in the fine print. Cancel&nbsp;
before the trial ends to avoid being charged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:58.080
but if we’re being honest, this is rather&nbsp;
misleading. The average user probably hears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.580
about Skillshare as this godly learning platform&nbsp;
on YouTube that you can try for one month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.680
completely for free. Then, they come to this&nbsp;
page, and they see all this advertising about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:14.100
the one free month. So, they decide to give it&nbsp;
a shot only to be charged $168 two weeks later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:19.680
And this doesn’t seem to be an honest marketing&nbsp;
mistake either. Given the number of complaints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.560
on TrustPilot, I’m sure their help center has&nbsp;
been overloaded with confused customers. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:31.560
they stick to the same slimy sales tactic. Worse,&nbsp;
it doesn’t seem like they try to make it up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.640
customers either. Sam, for example, missed&nbsp;
the refund window for an annual subscription&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:42.480
by just 4 days and he was refused any sort of&nbsp;
refund. Even if you do get a refund though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:48.660
you’re not out of the woods just yet because you&nbsp;
could be charged one year later. Take Roberta’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:53.340
daughter for instance. One year ago, she&nbsp;
signed up for a free trial. She too was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.560
surprised when she was suddenly charged an annual&nbsp;
subscription. Fortunately, she was able to contact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.080
customer support and get a refund and cancel&nbsp;
her membership. But, one year later, she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.120
charged for a renewal. When she contacted customer&nbsp;
support again, they told her that they can’t issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:15.600
a refund because they have a 1 refund policy which&nbsp;
she had already cashed in on. They even sent her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.340
a quote from her original email thread showing&nbsp;
that they had issued a refund. The funny part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:26.820
was that this quote also contained evidence that&nbsp;
she had asked to cancel one year ago. But, when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:32.640
this was pointed out to them, they simply stopped&nbsp;
replying. All of this is truly quite a shame for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:39.280
company that’s so good customer acquisition, but&nbsp;
this is simply the harsh reality of Skillshare.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:40.860
CONTENT CREATOR PAY:
If all of that wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:45.900
already bad enough, well it&nbsp;
gets even worse. Skillshare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.900
not only has subpar content that costs&nbsp;
a fortune, but they’re also preying on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.900 --> 00:10:00.480
YouTubers’ most loyal audience. When it comes&nbsp;
to Skillshare, I bet that a significant portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.460
of people who sign up aren’t even trying to&nbsp;
learn. They simply want to support a creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:11.460
and see more content from said creator similar&nbsp;
to something like Patreon. But, unlike Patreon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:17.040
most of the subscription fee doesn’t even end&nbsp;
up going to the creators. Patreon only keeps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:24.900
5 to 12% of the fees. Skillshare, on the other&nbsp;
hand, keeps wait for it 70%. This is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:30.960
unacceptable when you consider that YouTube itself&nbsp;
only takes 45%, and they’re content is available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:37.980
to end users for free. Meanwhile, Skillshare&nbsp;
is charging their users a $168 per year which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:44.100
is again even more than YouTube premium. Yet,&nbsp;
they’re only sharing 30% of it with the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:45.900
creators. This is stark contrast to virtually&nbsp;
everyone else in the space. Take Teachable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:51.960
for example. These guys have become the go to&nbsp;
place for hosting courses, and guess what? They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:56.520
have a free plan for creators that supports&nbsp;
unlimited students. This does come along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:02.640
a 10% fee per sale, but creators can get this&nbsp;
per sale fee down to 0 by upgrading tiers. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.500
Netflix, who people seem to hate, is extremely&nbsp;
generous with their revenue sharing. Much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:12.900
main Stranger Things cast for example was paid&nbsp;
a quarter million per episode. If all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.400
other businesses are able to stay afloat without&nbsp;
taking anywhere close to 70% of the revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:23.160
there’s no reason for Skillshare to be taking&nbsp;
70%. It appears to me that Skillshare figured out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:28.380
that many YouTubers have extremely loyal audiences&nbsp;
that are willing to pay up large amounts of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:33.240
to get more content and theoretically support&nbsp;
their favorite creators. Instead of creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:38.460
a smooth platform to enable such support though,&nbsp;
Skillshare has decided to cash in for themselves.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:47.220
THE TRUTH ABOUT SKILLSHARE:
In the end, YouTube is filled with glowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:52.380
endorsements of Skillshare, but much of this is&nbsp;
just due to Skillshare’s smart positioning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.300
branding. Behind the scenes though, Skillshare&nbsp;
doesn’t accomplish anything notably great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:02.460
In fact, they have multiple notable negative&nbsp;
attributes. They have pretty shady free trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:08.700
renewal, and cancellation policies, and they only&nbsp;
share 30% of their revenue with creators. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:13.320
even putting those attributes aside,&nbsp;
the bottom line is that Skillshare just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:17.820
isn’t worth it. You’re basically getting a&nbsp;
discount version of YouTube that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:22.260
to pay exorbitant fees for. If any other&nbsp;
company was offering this same service,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:27.720
they would’ve gotten shredded by the community.&nbsp;
But, because Skillshare is such a big player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:33.120
within the sponsorship scene, no YouTuber wants&nbsp;
to speak out against them. And audiences are led&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:38.040
to believe that Skillshare truly is some sort of&nbsp;
exceptional service that’s worth a premium price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:44.640
when in reality that’s just not the case.&nbsp;
But, that’s just what I think. Have you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:49.560
ever used Skillshare? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you appreciate the honest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.520
take on Skillshare. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.120
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

